\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{EDCR as a dual-layer diagnostic.}
The Evidence-Decoupling Ratio (\S\ref{sec:edcr}) provides diagnostic value at two complementary levels. At the method level, EDCR correlates strongly with extraction quality across optimization paradigms: Spearman $\rho = -0.965$ between EDCR and Rel-F1 (Table~\ref{tab:spectrum}). GRPO QLoRA reaches EDCR $= 0.9876$, confirming catastrophic decoupling; RSFT achieves $0.6503$ (four-seed mean), consistent with healthy joint optimization; SFT falls between at $0.6982$. The SFT baseline EDCR reflects the inherent cost of evidence generation; EDCR values substantially exceeding this baseline indicate RL-induced decoupling beyond what evidence production alone explains.

At the per-relation level, EDCR computed over 76 relation types ($\geq$5 samples each) correlates with Rel-F1 ($\rho = -0.793$, $p < 0.001$) but is independent of Evi-F1 ($\rho = -0.064$, $p = 0.58$), confirming that extraction difficulty and evidence quality are decoupled across relation types. This independence validates EDCR as a diagnostic that captures misalignment invisible to aggregate metrics (per-relation analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:edcr_per_relation}).


\paragraph{Why RSFT avoids decoupling.}
RSFT selects complete outputs rather than optimizing a differentiable reward signal, so there is no gradient pathway through which the model can trade extraction quality for evidence quality. Only outputs where both capabilities are jointly strong survive CED filtering. This structural property of discrete selection, rather than any RSFT-specific design, explains the absence of decoupling (detailed analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:rsft_theory}).


\paragraph{Scoring and selection ablation.}
CED and flat-NLI scoring produce comparable RSFT performance (mean Rel-F1 0.4563 vs.\ 0.4561); we adopt CED for its interpretable per-triple discrimination scores (full ablation in Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}). Stricter selection ($k{=}1$) outperforms $k{=}3$ by 1.02 Ign-F1 points despite 2.96$\times$ fewer training examples, confirming that scoring precision matters more than data volume.

